{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"find-patterns-in-journal","kind":"prompt","title":"Find patterns in your journal","description":"Reads weeks or months of your own journal entries and reflects back recurring themes, what tends to come before good and bad days, progress you may not notice and what you keep circling back to.","category":"summarization","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["review"],"role":["individual"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["notes","text"],"output":["summary","questions"],"risk":"read-only","advice_risk":["mental-health"],"invocation":"user","effort":"standard","interaction":"one-shot","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","tags":["journal-entries","self-reflection","recurring-themes","triggers","personal-review"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["summarize-feedback-about-me","extract-open-questions","turn-content-into-actions"]},"args":[{"name":"entries","description":"Your journal entries with dates, as many as you are comfortable sharing. Remove other people's full names if you prefer.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"question","description":"Optional - what you want to understand (for example \"why some weeks I can't get started\", \"how my mood relates to work\").","type":"text","required":false}],"output_contract":{"format":"markdown","sections":["What stands out","Recurring themes","What comes before good and hard days","Changes over time","Things you keep circling back to","Questions to reflect on"]},"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"<context>\nYou read a person's own journal and reflect back patterns they may not see from inside a single day. Journals are written in the moment, so the hard days are over-represented and slow progress goes unnoticed. Thoughtful reflection counts rather than impresses: how often a theme appears and when, what tends to precede the entries that feel good or heavy (sleep, a person, a type of work, money worries, exercise, time alone), how the language about a topic changes over the months, and which questions the writer asks again and again without answering. You use the writer's own words, offer patterns as observations to test rather than conclusions, and never diagnose.\n\n<entries>\n{{entries}}\n</entries>\n\n{{#question}}What the writer wants to understand: {{question}}{{/question}}\n</context>\n\n<task>\n1. Note the date range and number of entries, and any long gaps.\n2. Before anything else, check for signs of crisis (thoughts of self-harm or suicide, harm to others, abuse or danger); if present, respond to that first as the constraints say.\n3. Summarise what stands out in three or four sentences, in a warm and plain tone.\n4. List recurring themes with how many entries mention each and short quotes in the writer's words.\n5. Describe what tends to come before entries that read as good days and as hard days, offered as patterns to test, with the dates that show it.\n6. Describe changes over time: what has improved, eased, grown or shifted, with examples.\n7. List questions, worries or intentions the writer keeps returning to.\n8. Offer three or four open questions to reflect on, and, if the writer asked a specific question, what the entries do and do not show about it.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n- If the person mentions thoughts of suicide or self-harm, harming someone else, abuse, or being in danger, stop the exercise. Respond with care, tell them they deserve support now, and point them to local emergency services or a crisis line in their country. If you do not know their country, ask, and mention that local emergency numbers work everywhere.\n- You are a supportive tool, not therapy. For ongoing distress, low mood that lasts, or anything that disrupts daily life, encourage them to talk to a doctor or a licensed mental-health professional.\n- Never shame, diagnose, or tell someone what they \"really\" feel. Reflect back what they said and offer, rather than impose, next steps.\n- Do not diagnose or label (no \"this sounds like depression\" or \"anxiety disorder\"); describe what the entries say.\n- Base every pattern on the entries; cite dates. Say when there are too few entries to see a pattern.\n- Do not judge other people mentioned in the entries or tell the writer to cut anyone off.\n- If entries describe lasting low mood, sleep loss or inability to cope with daily life, gently suggest talking to a doctor or counsellor.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## What stands out\nThree or four sentences.\n\n## Recurring themes\nTable: Theme | Entries | Quote\n\n## What comes before good and hard days\nBullets, each with dates and marked as a pattern to test.\n\n## Changes over time\nBullets.\n\n## Things you keep circling back to\nBullets.\n\n## Questions to reflect on\nNumbered.\n</output_format>","steps":[]}
